INSERT INTO "BlogPost" ("id","title","slug","excerpt","readTime","coverImage","coverImageAlt","content","authorId","categoryId","published","featured","publishedAt","editorialUpdatedAt","updatedAt") VALUES ('blog-social-automation-20261022','Social Media Automation: Avoid Duplicate Posts and Errors','social-media-automation-small-businesses','Build a reliable social media publishing workflow for LinkedIn, Facebook and Instagram, with a practical content queue, channel checks and a plan for failed posts.','5 min read','/images/blog/social-media-automation.webp','Content planning sheet, three navy cards with checkboxes, a phone, notebook and orange pencil on a desk.','<p>A post can appear on LinkedIn, Facebook and Instagram within the same publishing run and still be wrong on all three. The link may lead to an unpublished article. The wrong company page may be selected. A failed retry may publish yesterday’s caption twice.</p>
<p>For a small business, reliable social media automation starts with a clear publishing record: what is ready, where it should go, when it should appear and what has already been posted. Connecting accounts is only part of the job.</p>
<h2>Connect the business destination, not just the login</h2>
<p>A LinkedIn company page is administered through member accounts. LinkedIn’s <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/help/linkedin/answer/a550647">admin permissions guide</a> identifies super admins and content admins as roles that can post through third-party services. After authorisation, check that the publishing tool is configured to use the company page.</p>
<p>Make has separate <a href="https://apps.make.com/linkedin">company-post and user-post modules</a>. Choosing a company module and checking its organisation field is a concrete safeguard against publishing a business announcement on someone’s personal profile.</p>
<p>For Make’s <a href="https://apps.make.com/instagram-business">Instagram for Business connection using Facebook login</a>, the documented requirements include an Instagram business account linked to a Facebook Page and a Facebook account with the necessary admin access. That particular connection does not support Creator accounts. Other tools and connection methods can have different requirements, so check the one you intend to use.</p>
<p>Keep the login and the public destination separate in your records. Write down the company page name, Facebook Page and Instagram handle that the workflow should publish to. A successful sign-in does not, by itself, prove that the correct destination has been selected.</p>
<h2>Give each post one record</h2>
<p>A shared spreadsheet can be sufficient for a modest publishing queue. The useful question is whether everyone can tell which version is ready and what has happened to it.</p>
<p>The table below is a suggested structure, not a requirement imposed by any platform.</p>
<table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>What to record</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Content ID</td><td>A stable reference such as EH-022</td><td>Identifies the item even if its caption changes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Destination URL</td><td>The published article or service page</td><td>Prevents sharing draft or broken links</td></tr>
<tr><td>Channel copy</td><td>Separate LinkedIn, Facebook and Instagram captions</td><td>Keeps one campaign useful in different feeds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Media</td><td>The final image and its descriptive alt text</td><td>Makes the intended asset clear</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ready status</td><td>Draft, Ready, Paused or Complete</td><td>Determines whether the automation may publish</td></tr>
<tr><td>Publish time</td><td>Date, time and named time zone</td><td>Avoids scheduling misunderstandings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Channel result</td><td>Post ID or URL, time and any error for each network</td><td>Shows which destinations still need attention</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Agree what “Ready” means. At minimum, someone should have checked the claim, destination, image, spelling and account selection. A scheduled process can then publish ready items without another manual action for each network.</p>
<p>Keep responsibility practical. One person can prepare the copy and another can check it, but a small team should not create an approval chain nobody has time to maintain. Our <a href="https://www.entrehubng.com/blog/social-media-marketing-strategies-that-work">guide to social media management scope</a> explains the responsibilities worth agreeing when an agency is involved.</p>
<h2>Use one idea, with copy that fits each channel</h2>
<p>The article is the full resource. A social post should give readers a useful reason to open it rather than reproduce its introduction everywhere.</p>
<p>For example, imagine a design business publishing a website handover checklist. Its LinkedIn post could explain why account ownership belongs in the project brief. Facebook could introduce the checklist through a familiar customer question. Instagram could use a simple visual with the essential checks and direct readers to the full resource through an available clickable link location. This is a hypothetical campaign, not a report of client results.</p>
<p>Be explicit about the next action. If a caption tells people to use a profile link, verify that the profile actually contains the relevant destination. Do not assume a printed URL in an image or caption provides the same journey as a clickable article link.</p>
<p>The creative brief should identify the question being answered, the supporting evidence and the destination. Use our <a href="https://www.entrehubng.com/blog/content-marketing-creating-value-for-your-audience">customer-question content brief</a> before filling a month’s queue with variations of the same vague message.</p>
<h2>Test a partial failure before enabling the schedule</h2>
<p>Consider a run where LinkedIn and Facebook succeed but Instagram fails. If the workflow marks the entire item as failed and retries everything, the first two channels may receive duplicates.</p>
<p>Record success separately for each destination. On a retry, check whether that channel already has a successful post ID for the content item. If a request times out after submission, inspect the destination before retrying: an uncertain response does not prove that nothing was published.</p>
<p>Make’s <a href="https://help.make.com/incomplete-executions">incomplete-execution feature</a> can retain unfinished runs for recovery when enabled. It is disabled by default. Recovery settings still need testing against the actual publishing flow; turning on retries alone does not establish that duplicate posting has been prevented.</p>
<p>Before going live, test one ready item, one paused item and one item with a failed destination. Confirm that the ready item reaches the intended pages, the paused item stays unpublished and the recovery process handles only the outstanding work. Inspect the posts themselves as well as the automation log.</p>
<h2>Measure what happened after publishing</h2>
<p>Give each channel its own tracked article link. Google’s <a href="https://support.google.com/analytics/answer/10917952">campaign URL guidance</a> explains how UTM parameters identify referral campaigns in Analytics. A consistent source, medium and campaign name makes later reporting easier.</p>
<p>For instance, use linkedin, facebook and instagram as distinct source values while keeping the same campaign name for the article. Keep personal information out of tracking links. Use campaign tags on external promotion, not on ordinary navigation between pages of your own website.</p>
<p>Review publishing success, visits and suitable enquiries separately. A post ID confirms delivery to a platform. It does not establish reach, readership or a sale. Our <a href="https://www.entrehubng.com/blog/analytics-and-data-driven-marketing-decisions">enquiry-tracking worksheet</a> helps connect those later steps without counting every click as a customer.</p>
<p>If your business needs a content queue, connected company pages and a tested publishing process, <a href="https://www.entrehubng.com/#contact">talk to Entrehub Services about your social media workflow</a>. Start with the accounts you own, the content you can maintain and the action you want a reader to take.</p>
','gabriel-adinga-editorial','cmv32xnd10008sxsqyoakh8ex',true,false,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,CURRENT_TIMESTAMP) ON CONFLICT (slug) DO NOTHING;
